meta {
  name: Update Role
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/roles/{{createdRoleId}}
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{ownerAccessToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "description": "Renamed and re-scoped by the Bruno collection.",
    "dataScope": "own",
    "levels": [
      { "moduleKey": "leads", "level": "write" },
      { "moduleKey": "dashboard", "level": "read" }
    ],
    "adminPermissions": ["agency:changelogs:read"]
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("write implies read", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().permissions).to.include.members([
      "leads:read",
      "leads:write",
      "dashboard:read",
    ]);
  });

  test("applies the metadata too", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().dataScope).to.equal("own");
  });

  test("grants the agency capability", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().permissions).to.include("agency:changelogs:read");
  });

  test("never grants a platform permission", function () {
    const platform = res
      .getBody()
      .permissions.filter((p) => p.startsWith("platform:"));
    expect(platform).to.eql([]);
  });
}

docs {
  # PATCH /roles/:roleId — update a role

  **Module:** none (`@SkipModule()`).
  **Permission:** `agency:roles:write`

  One endpoint takes both the role's metadata and its page-permission matrix,
  and applies whichever parts are present. `{ levels }` alone is the shape the
  web has always sent, so this stays backward compatible.

  ## Body — all fields optional

  | Field | Type | Notes |
  |---|---|---|
  | name | string, 2–60 | A **system role** can be renamed for display, but its slug is referenced by code and never changes. |
  | description | string, ≤240 | |
  | dataScope | `own` \| `branch` \| `agency` | Changing it invalidates every holder's cached permissions — a stale scope is a user reading rows they should no longer see. |
  | levels | `{ moduleKey, level }[]` | `level` is `none` \| `read` \| `write`. Replaces the whole page matrix. |
  | adminPermissions | `string[]` | The `agency:*` capabilities the role grants. Replaces the whole set. |

  ## Both permission fields are independently optional

  **Omitting one preserves it; sending `[]` clears it.** So `{ levels }` alone —
  which is what the web sent for its whole life — leaves the role's capabilities
  untouched, and `{ adminPermissions }` alone leaves the page matrix untouched.
  The checks are for `undefined` rather than falsiness precisely so an empty
  array stays a real instruction.

  ⚠ `platform:*` is preserved and **never settable here**, whatever is passed.
  Those permissions sit above the tenant boundary; an agency owner granting
  themselves one would be a privilege escalation out of their own tenant.
  Passing one is a `400` rather than a silent drop — a silently ignored
  permission would show in the UI as saved when it was never stored.

  ## How levels are applied

  - `write` expands to both `{module}:read` and `{module}:write`; `write` always
    implies `read`.
  - Levels for modules the agency has **not** enabled are dropped silently — the
    permission would be filtered out at resolution time anyway.
  - Existing `agency:*` / `platform:*` permissions on the role are **preserved**
    and cannot be added or removed here. Admin capabilities are not part of the
    page model.
  - Every holder's cached permission set is invalidated.

  ## Errors
  - `404 Not Found` — no such role in this agency.
  - `409 Conflict` — the role has `grantsAllEnabledModules` (Agency Owner). Its
    access is a *rule* — everything the agency has enabled — not a stored matrix,
    so editing its levels would either do nothing or appear to work. The web has
    always rendered it read-only; the API used to disagree, which let an owner
    PATCH their own admin permissions away.
  - `409 Conflict` — renaming a system role in a way that would change its slug.
  - `403 Forbidden` — missing `agency:roles:write`.
}
